Date: Sat, 19 Jun 93 12:03:24 PDT
From: "Mark W. McFadden" <mwm@wwtc.timeplex.com>
Subject: Scientific validity in metaprogramming experiments

On Mon, 19 Jul 93 19:27:24 +0100, Rich Walker wrote:

>I would suspect that the approach to take would involve checking 
>beforehand what the expectations of the participants were, if they were
>told what they were getting into. Then, afterwards, check if the
>participants believed it was going to work. Only problem is ensuring
>they're telling the truth. (Discreet questioning separately from someone
>who obviously believes in it, an arch-skeptic, and someone who's
>impartial?) 

>Rich!
 
 Good point. I'd suggest one slight change, but it kind of snowballs. I 
don't think _expecting_ something to work or _believing_ something will 
work is quite the same as _wanting_ it to work. I don't know if this 
distinction should be implicit in the design of an experiment, or if it 
should be part of another experiment entirely. 
 Perhaps one variation of the experiment should include competition between 
the groups, each group trying to produce more or better results or higher 
scores (incentive? Each group is composed of people convinced they are 
"right"). I'm not sure an experiment (ritual) performed by a disinterested 
or neutral participant would produce anything. But once again, this just 
seems to suggest another experiment.
 
Perhaps something like:
 
Group 1a
 Practising mystics. Believers.

Group 1b
 Neutral group doing 1a ritual from instructions. (Much practice assumed)

Group 2a
 Metaprogramming group (Jungian test). Perform 1a ritual but replace 
deities with Pop culture analogs. Believers.

Group 2b
 Neutral metaprogramming group. 2a ritual, no expectations (hard to find).

Group 3a
 Metaprogramming group (secular/Skinner). Design own ritual. Believers.

Group 3b
 Neutral metaprogramming group. 3a ritual, no expectations.

Group 4a
 Skeptics. Try to reproduce results using biofeedback etc. Design own 
tests.

Group 4b
 Neutral control. Perform same exercises as 4a. No expectations.


 Observers should be neutral.
