Date: Mon, 19 Jul 93 17:33:05 PDT
From: Robin Hanson <hanson@ptolemy.arc.nasa.gov>
Subject: Wage Competition (was: What are big upcoming problems?)

>From the discussion so far, it seems folk's biggest worries are wage
competition and state controlled computer networks.  So here I start a
thread devoted to solving the first problem.

People are worried about automation/robots and upload copies competing
for their jobs.  Sorta an echo of NAFTA worries, I guess.  

Basic economics suggests that in a reasonably competitive labor
market, folks should earn a wage about equal to their marginal product
(how much more wealth is produced if they work than if they don't).
So the first cut of an answer is to say that as long as you can keep
just as productive, you should earn the same real wage.  If robots or
Mexicans or whatever get even more productive, they earn more, but
unless the issue is envy, you needn't worry.

Of course if rising wages of others makes real prices for things you
value like real estate rise, you may have to settle for being
literally shoved to the side of the main action.  And if you think you
are getting paid more than you are worth now because of some monopoly
position (government job, seniority, immigration restrictions), then
of course you may worry about more competition :-).

But this still isn't the whole story, since your marginal productivity
depends on supporting infastructure, and so may decline dramatically
if that infastructure is displaced by a new one.  So if you are a
productive PC programmer only because there are tons of PCs out there,
and then there aren't tons of PCs, you might be in trouble if you
didn't bother to switch along with everyone else.  Or if you would be
a great cog in a 50s style megacorp, but can't work well with folks in
the new style corp, you might earn less, in real terms, than you did
in the 50s.

But if you are flexible, and learn as quick as the next guy to learn
to do whatever is in demand, you should be safe, right?  Well, there
are still two more problems.  

First, what if the ratio of the return to capital to the return to
labor, which hasn't changed that much over the centuries, suddenly
drops?  That is, what if capital, in the form of robots, machines,
software, etc. earns most of the returns from production, due to a
might higher marginal product?  Then if you had more than the average
percentage of your wealth tied up in your ability to sell your labor,
rather than owning machines, etc., you might experience a net, perhaps
large, drop in wealth.

A solution to this is simply to help folks diversify their assets
more.  Poor folk, who now tend to have more of their assets in their
future wage potential, might be well advised to work harder to save
and invest more, either by consuming less or by selling the rights to
some of their future labor.

A different problem might come from a sudden increase in the variance
of wages.  For example, what if a couple of the first hundred uploads
to take up some profession usually managed to fill the niche for that
type of work, making however many copies the market could bear and
using their brand name power and extra learning time to keep out
newcomers?  While average wages might remain high, even relative to
total wealth, most people might lose most of their wage-earning power.

Again the only reasonable solution I see is to support diversification
of assets.  If we don't now know who will succeed, and sell each other
percentages of our future wages, we might greatly reduce our risk, at
some moderate cost due to reduced incentives to work hard.

Thus creating more explicit human capital markets, wherein folks could
buy and sell fractions of their future wages, might go a long way to
solving potential problems due to future wage competition.

Robin Hanson

